      "renderer": "manim",
      "narration": "Let's work through an example using the Ideal Gas Law, PV = nRT. Imagine we have a container with a volume of 10 litres. Inside, we have 2 moles of an ideal gas. The temperature of the gas is 300 Kelvin, which is about 27 degrees Celsius. Our goal is to find the pressure of the gas inside the container. The ideal gas constant, R, has a value of approximately 0.0821 litre-atmospheres per mole-Kelvin. First, we write down our known values: V equals 10 L, n equals 2 mol, T equals 300 K, and R equals 0.0821. Our unknown is the pressure, P. The formula is PV = nRT. To solve for P, we can rearrange the formula by dividing both sides by V. This gives us P = (nRT) / V. Now we can substitute our known values into this rearranged formula. So, P equals (2 moles times 0.0821 times 300 Kelvin) all divided by 10 litres. Let's calculate the top part first: 2 times 0.0821 times 300 equals 49.26. So the equation becomes P = 49.26 divided by 10. This gives us a final pressure of 4.926 atmospheres. So, by using the Ideal Gas Law, we were able to calculate the pressure of the gas based on its volume, temperature, and the amount of gas present.",
