      "title": "Example: Area of a Circle",
      "renderer": "manim",
      "narration": "Let's calculate the area of a circle. Suppose we have a circular pizza with a radius of 7 centimetres. We want to find out the total area of the pizza to know how much we're about to eat! First, we identify our knowns. The radius, 'r', is 7 cm. The formula we need is A = πr². We will use the approximation of π as 3.14 for this calculation. Now we substitute the value of the radius into the formula. This gives us A equals 3.14 times 7 centimetres squared. According to the order of operations, we must do the exponent first. So, we calculate 7 squared, which is 7 times 7, giving us 49. The unit also gets squared, so we have 49 square centimetres. Now our equation is A equals 3.14 times 49 square centimetres. Finally, we perform the multiplication: 3.14 times 49 is 153.86. So, the area of our pizza is 153.86 square centimetres. By simply knowing the radius, we can find the exact area of any circle.",
